{
  "id": "gluconeogenesis",
  "name": "Gluconeogenesis",
  "category": "metabolic",
  "topic": "metabolism",
  "summary": "Liver (and kidney) synthesise glucose from pyruvate, lactate, glycerol and glucogenic amino acids. It reverses glycolysis but bypasses its three irreversible steps with four different enzymes.",
  "evidence": "established",
  "layout": "chain",
  "location": "Mitochondria (pyruvate carboxylase) → cytosol → ER lumen (glucose-6-phosphatase)",
  "net": "2 Pyruvate + 4 ATP + 2 GTP + 2 NADH + 6 H₂O → Glucose + 4 ADP + 2 GDP + 6 Pᵢ + 2 NAD⁺",
  "clinical": "Von Gierke disease (glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency, GSD Ia) causes fasting hypoglycaemia and lactic acidosis. Metformin lowers hepatic glucose output. Alcohol inhibits gluconeogenesis by raising NADH/NAD⁺.",
